From f3b27261d648c934360372e94318ccaff9d982f9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: vita-build <vita@build>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 20:59:53 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] Vita: the texture cache drawn from the texels it wrote
 (in-place textures in graphics memory of our own), a check of the cache each
 frame, dumps written in the background

The menus' broken letters, stray marks and stripes with the texture cache on (t11, t12): every
cached texel was drawn from the texel 6 to its left. Comparing the same menu's cache-on and
cache-off frames with the VRAM dump, each restored background piece, glyph and arrow showed the
texels 6 to the left on every page and both page rows (VRAM x 775 for 781). So letters lost their
right edge and showed a sliver of the glyph beside them in VRAM, and a page's first 6 columns
came from the slot beside it in the atlas (the green and red bars). The slots were written where
vitaGL's pointer to the 4096x2048 texture's texels said; the GPU read them 24 bytes before.

The atlas, the texture store's copies and the blit and movie-picture pools are now textures whose
texels are a 4 KB-aligned block of vitaGL's graphics memory (vglAlloc, CDRAM). vitaGL's
descriptor of an 8x8 placeholder is pointed at that block (sceGxmTextureSetData, SetWidth,
SetHeight), so the address written is the address read. Each frame the descriptors are checked:
one vitaGL rewrote is pointed back and logged. Four rows of the decoded slots are compared with a
fresh decode: a slot that missed a write is freed and counted as stale. When every store copy was
drawn in the last 4 frames, a write now waits for the GPU (glFinish) instead of vitaGL's copy.

The decode expands 5-bit channels as n * 255 / 31, rounded. The old n << 3 | n >> 2 is up to 0.6
less, so cached pixels read back a step darker than the emulated lookup's.

THPS_SHOT: a dump is made in memory and written by its own thread in 256 KB pieces. The game
thread's small writes of a 1.5 MB dump froze the game at frame 1500. While 4 dumps are still being
written, a new one is skipped. The [gl] line counts stale slots, descriptors pointed back and
waits for the GPU. Start-up logs where the atlas's and the base target's texels are, and why
in-place writes are off when they are.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
